## 5. Run Shapiro-Wilk on bill length

Run `shapiro.test()` on the non-missing bill-length values in `bill_data`. Save the result as `shapiro_length` and print it.

::: {.callout-note appearance="simple"}
## Reading Shapiro-Wilk output

The null hypothesis is **normality**. A **small p-value** (conventionally below 0.05) means the data look **inconsistent with** a normal distribution. A **large p-value** means you do **not** have evidence against normality — not proof that the data are perfectly normal.
:::




::: {.cell envir='assumptions_lab' exercise='warmup_shapiro'}
```{webr}
#| envir: assumptions_lab
#| exercise: warmup_shapiro
shapiro_length <- 

```
:::




::: {.hint exercise="warmup_shapiro"}
::: {.callout-note collapse="false"}
## Hint

From `?shapiro.test`: pass a numeric vector directly.

```r
shapiro.test(mtcars$mpg)
```

Use the bill-length column from `bill_data`.
:::
:::

::: {.solution exercise="warmup_shapiro"}
::: {.callout-tip collapse="false"}
## Solution

```r
shapiro_length <- shapiro.test(bill_data$bill_length_mm)
shapiro_length
```

You should see p ≈ 0.69 — no evidence against normality for bill length.
:::
:::




::: {.cell envir='assumptions_lab' exercise='warmup_shapiro' check='true'}
```{webr}
#| envir: assumptions_lab
#| exercise: warmup_shapiro
#| check: true
code <- paste(.user_code, collapse = "\n")
if (!grepl("shapiro\\.test\\s*\\(", code)) {
  list(
    correct = FALSE,
    type = "error",
    message = "Call `shapiro.test()` on Adelie bill length."
  )
} else if (!exists("shapiro_length", envir = .envir_result, inherits = FALSE)) {
  list(
    correct = FALSE,
    type = "warning",
    message = "Save the result as `shapiro_length`."
  )
} else {
  sl <- get("shapiro_length", envir = .envir_result)
  if (!is.null(sl$p.value) && sl$p.value > 0.05) {
    list(
      correct = TRUE,
      type = "success",
      message = paste0("p = ", signif(sl$p.value, 3), " — no evidence against normality. Good warm-up.")
    )
  } else {
    list(
      correct = TRUE,
      type = "success",
      message = "Shapiro-Wilk ran. Compare your p-value to 0.05."
    )
  }
}
```
:::




## 6. Mean bill area with missing values

A published survey of a neighboring Adelie population reported a mean bill area of **700 mm²**. Calculate the mean `bill_area` across all penguins in your sample and save it as `mean_raw`. Use `na.rm = TRUE`. Print the result and compare it to the reference value.




::: {.cell envir='assumptions_lab' exercise='mean_raw'}
```{webr}
#| envir: assumptions_lab
#| exercise: mean_raw
mean_raw <- 

```
:::




::: {.hint exercise="mean_raw"}
::: {.callout-note collapse="false"}
## Hint

From `?mean`: `mean(x, na.rm = FALSE)` — set `na.rm = TRUE` to skip missing values.

```r
mean(mtcars$mpg, na.rm = TRUE)
```

Apply to `bill_data$bill_area`.
:::
:::

::: {.solution exercise="mean_raw"}
::: {.callout-tip collapse="false"}
## Solution

```r
mean_raw <- mean(bill_data$bill_area, na.rm = TRUE)
mean_raw
```

You should see about 729.6 mm² — a bit above the reference value of 700.
:::
:::




::: {.cell envir='assumptions_lab' exercise='mean_raw' check='true'}
```{webr}
#| envir: assumptions_lab
#| exercise: mean_raw
#| check: true
code <- paste(.user_code, collapse = "\n")
if (!grepl("mean\\s*\\(", code)) {
  list(
    correct = FALSE,
    type = "error",
    message = "Use `mean()` on `bill_data$bill_area`."
  )
} else if (!grepl("na\\.rm\\s*=\\s*TRUE", code, ignore.case = TRUE)) {
  list(
    correct = FALSE,
    type = "info",
    message = "Include `na.rm = TRUE` inside `mean()`."
  )
} else if (!exists("mean_raw", envir = .envir_result, inherits = FALSE)) {
  list(
    correct = FALSE,
    type = "warning",
    message = "Save the result as `mean_raw`."
  )
} else {
  mr <- get("mean_raw", envir = .envir_result)
  if (is.numeric(mr) && length(mr) == 1 && !is.na(mr) && mr > 720 && mr < 740) {
    list(
      correct = TRUE,
      type = "success",
      message = paste0("Mean ≈ ", round(mr, 1), " mm² — above the reference of 700.")
    )
  } else {
    list(
      correct = FALSE,
      type = "info",
      message = "Compute `mean_raw <- mean(bill_data$bill_area, na.rm = TRUE)`."
    )
  }
}
```
:::




## 7. Trim impossible values

**Step 1 — trim impossible values.** Bill area cannot be negative. Remove rows where `bill_area` is negative (a sign error). **Keep** rows where `bill_area` is missing. Save the result as `bill_clean`.

::: {.callout-note appearance="simple"}
## `|` means "or"

Inside `filter()`, the vertical bar **`|`** means **"or"**. The condition `is.na(bill_area) | bill_area >= 0` keeps a row if **either** the area is missing **or** it is zero or positive.

Writing `filter(bill_area >= 0)` alone silently **drops the missing rows** — because `NA >= 0` evaluates to `NA`, not `TRUE`.
:::




::: {.cell envir='assumptions_lab' exercise='trim_impossible'}
```{webr}
#| envir: assumptions_lab
#| exercise: trim_impossible
bill_clean <- bill_data |>

```
:::




::: {.hint exercise="trim_impossible"}
::: {.callout-note collapse="false"}
## Hint

From `?filter`: keep rows where conditions are TRUE.

```r
mtcars |>
  filter(is.na(mpg) | mpg >= 0)
```

Keep missing bill areas **or** non-negative areas.
:::
:::

::: {.solution exercise="trim_impossible"}
::: {.callout-tip collapse="false"}
## Solution

```r
bill_clean <- bill_data |>
  filter(is.na(bill_area) | bill_area >= 0)
```

50 rows — no negative values in this dataset, so nothing was removed. The one missing measurement is still there. **Confirming there are no impossible values is itself a reportable result.**
:::
:::




::: {.cell envir='assumptions_lab' exercise='trim_impossible' check='true'}
```{webr}
#| envir: assumptions_lab
#| exercise: trim_impossible
#| check: true
code <- paste(.user_code, collapse = "\n")
if (!exists("bill_clean", envir = .envir_result, inherits = FALSE)) {
  list(
    correct = FALSE,
    type = "warning",
    message = "Save the filtered table as `bill_clean`."
  )
} else {
  bc <- get("bill_clean", envir = .envir_result)
  n <- nrow(bc)
  n_na <- sum(is.na(bc$bill_area))
  n_neg <- sum(!is.na(bc$bill_area) & bc$bill_area < 0)
  used_or <- grepl("is\\.na\\s*\\(\\s*bill_area\\s*\\)", code) && grepl("\\|", code)
  if (n == 50 && n_na == 1 && n_neg == 0) {
    list(
      correct = TRUE,
      type = "success",
      message = "50 rows — no negatives found, missing value kept. That is the correct outcome here."
    )
  } else if (n == 49 && n_na == 0) {
    list(
      correct = FALSE,
      type = "error",
      message = "`filter(bill_area >= 0)` alone drops missing rows. Use `is.na(bill_area) | bill_area >= 0`."
    )
  } else if (!used_or) {
    list(
      correct = FALSE,
      type = "info",
      message = "Use `filter(is.na(bill_area) | bill_area >= 0)` so missing rows stay."
    )
  } else {
    list(
      correct = FALSE,
      type = "info",
      message = "Filter with `is.na(bill_area) | bill_area >= 0` and save as `bill_clean`."
    )
  }
}
```
:::




## 8. Flag distributional outliers

**Step 2 — flag outliers.** Using only the non-missing bill areas in `bill_clean`, calculate the upper fence of the 1.5 × IQR rule: Q3 + 1.5 × IQR. Save it as `upper_fence` and print it. Also count how many non-missing bill areas exceed that fence and save the count as `n_raw_outliers`. Print both values. Do not remove any rows.




::: {.cell envir='assumptions_lab' setup='true' exercise='upper_fence'}
```{webr}
#| envir: assumptions_lab
#| setup: true
#| exercise: upper_fence
library(tidyverse)
library(palmerpenguins)
bill_data <- penguins |>
  filter(species == "Adelie", year == 2007) |>
  mutate(bill_area = bill_length_mm * bill_depth_mm)
bill_clean <- bill_data |>
  filter(is.na(bill_area) | bill_area >= 0)
```
:::

::: {.cell envir='assumptions_lab' exercise='upper_fence'}
```{webr}
#| envir: assumptions_lab
#| exercise: upper_fence
upper_fence <- 

```
:::




::: {.hint exercise="upper_fence"}
::: {.callout-note collapse="false"}
## Hint

From `?quantile`: `quantile(x, probs, na.rm = FALSE)`. From `?IQR`: `IQR(x, na.rm = FALSE)`.

```r
q3 <- quantile(mtcars$mpg, 0.75, na.rm = TRUE)
fence <- q3 + 1.5 * IQR(mtcars$mpg, na.rm = TRUE)
n_out <- sum(mtcars$mpg > fence, na.rm = TRUE)
```

Use the 75th percentile and IQR of non-missing bill areas, then count values above the fence.
:::
:::

::: {.solution exercise="upper_fence"}
::: {.callout-tip collapse="false"}
## Solution

```r
upper_fence <- quantile(bill_clean$bill_area, 0.75, na.rm = TRUE) +
  1.5 * IQR(bill_clean$bill_area, na.rm = TRUE)
n_raw_outliers <- sum(bill_clean$bill_area > upper_fence, na.rm = TRUE)
upper_fence
n_raw_outliers
```

The upper fence is about 920 mm² — one penguin has a bill area above it.
:::
:::




::: {.cell envir='assumptions_lab' exercise='upper_fence' check='true'}
```{webr}
#| envir: assumptions_lab
#| exercise: upper_fence
#| check: true
code <- paste(.user_code, collapse = "\n")
if (!exists("upper_fence", envir = .envir_result, inherits = FALSE)) {
  list(
    correct = FALSE,
    type = "warning",
    message = "Save the result as `upper_fence`."
  )
} else {
  uf <- as.numeric(get("upper_fence", envir = .envir_result))
  has_q <- grepl("quantile\\s*\\(", code)
  has_iqr <- grepl("IQR\\s*\\(", code)
  has_count <- grepl("n_raw_outliers", code) && grepl("sum\\s*\\(", code)
  n_out <- if (exists("n_raw_outliers", envir = .envir_result, inherits = FALSE)) {
    get("n_raw_outliers", envir = .envir_result)
  } else NA
  if (length(uf) == 1 && abs(uf - 919.76) < 1 && has_q && has_iqr && !is.na(n_out) && n_out == 1) {
    list(
      correct = TRUE,
      type = "success",
      message = "Upper fence ≈ 920 mm². One bill area sits above it — flag it, do not delete it yet."
    )
  } else if (has_q && has_iqr && !is.na(n_out) && n_out == 1) {
    list(
      correct = TRUE,
      type = "success",
      message = paste0("Upper fence ≈ ", round(uf, 1), " mm² with ", n_out, " outlier. Good.")
    )
  } else if (!has_count) {
    list(
      correct = FALSE,
      type = "info",
      message = "Also count outliers: `n_raw_outliers <- sum(bill_clean$bill_area > upper_fence, na.rm = TRUE)`."
    )
  } else {
    list(
      correct = FALSE,
      type = "info",
      message = "Use `quantile(..., 0.75, na.rm = TRUE) + 1.5 * IQR(..., na.rm = TRUE)` and count values above the fence."
    )
  }
}
```
:::




## 9. Histogram on the raw scale

**Step 3 — assess normality.** Make a histogram of the non-missing bill areas in `bill_clean` using `ggplot()` and `geom_histogram()`. Save the plot as `hist_raw` and display it.




::: {.cell envir='assumptions_lab' exercise='hist_raw'}
```{webr}
#| envir: assumptions_lab
#| exercise: hist_raw
hist_raw <- ggplot(bill_clean, aes(x = bill_area)) +

```
:::




::: {.hint exercise="hist_raw"}
::: {.callout-note collapse="false"}
## Hint

From `?ggplot`: add a layer with `+`.

```r
hist_raw <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(x = mpg)) +
  geom_histogram()
hist_raw
```

Map `bill_area` to the x-axis and assign the whole plot to `hist_raw`.
:::
:::

::: {.solution exercise="hist_raw"}
::: {.callout-tip collapse="false"}
